---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile, workbench } from '../../data/site';
---

<Base title={workbench.title} description={workbench.description}>
  <article class="project-detail">
    <header class="detail-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Personal project · Design overview</p>
      <h1>{workbench.title}</h1>
      <p class="lede">{workbench.description}</p>
    </header>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>How it <em>works</em></h2>
      <p>
        The proposed workbench frames model selection as an empirical platform decision. For each workload,
        define the expected outcome, evaluate candidate models under recorded conditions, and relate
        observed quality to serving cost. The result is a decision teams can inspect and revisit as
        models, workloads, and constraints change.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Operating principle:</strong> recommend from measured outcomes and make the
          evaluation context visible. A score without its workload and conditions is not a useful
          platform decision.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Evaluation <em>tracks</em></h2>
      <div class="track-grid">
        {workbench.tracks.map((track, index) => (
          <article class="track-card">
            <p class="eyebrow">Track 0{index + 1}</p>
            <h3>{track.title}</h3>
            <p>{track.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Key design <em>decisions</em></h2>
      <div class="decision-list">
        {workbench.decisions.map((decision, index) => (
          <div class="decision-row">
            <span class="decision-number">0{index + 1}</span>
            <div><h3>{decision.title}</h3><p>{decision.body}</p></div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Trust and <em>governance</em></h2>
      <p>
        Enterprise adoption depends on more than a model score. The evaluation design should make
        inputs, source grounding, action constraints, and outcome provenance inspectable. For
        enterprise RAG, permission-aware access and the evidence behind an answer are central
        requirements; they should be evaluated as part of the workload rather than treated as
        deployment details.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Extensibility</h2>
      <p>
        A new model should be introduced through a recorded configuration; a new use case should
        arrive with its own task suite and success criteria. This keeps comparisons legible as the
        workbench grows and avoids silently changing what a score means.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Measured <em>results</em></h2>
      <div class="todo-note">
        TODO: Add measured benchmark results after runs are available. Include the workload, model
        configuration, evaluation conditions, task quality, and serving cost for every reported
        comparison. Do not infer or estimate results.
      </div>
      <table class="spec">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Quality</th><td>Measured task success for each evaluation track</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Economics</th><td>Serving cost associated with each measured configuration</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Traceability</th><td>Workload, model, configuration, and run conditions</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Technical <em>architecture</em></h2>
      <p>
        The design separates workload definitions, model configurations, evaluation runs, and
        result reporting. That boundary allows teams to add models and tasks independently while
        preserving the context behind each comparison. Implementation details and measured results
        belong here once they are verified from the project itself.
      </p>
      <table class="spec">
        <tbody>
          {workbench.stack.map((item) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{item.label}</th><td>{item.value}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <nav class="detail-navigation" aria-label="Project navigation">
      <a href="/#projects">← Back to projects</a>
      <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Discuss platform leadership →</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>
